<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Formulating and evaluating simultaneous capacity constraints of a capentry workshop</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A community furniture workshop in Embu specializes in manufacturing wooden dining chairs (\(x\)) and wooden dining tables (\(y\)). The daily production is limited by both available materials and available labor hours.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Timber:</b> Each chair requires <b>{@A@} meters</b> of timber, and each table requires <b>{@B@} meters</b> of timber. The workshop has a maximum of <b>{@W@} meters</b> of timber available per day.</li>
<li><b>Labor:</b> Each chair requires <b>{@C_coef@} hours</b> of labor, and each table requires <b>{@D@} hours</b> of labor. The workshop staff provides a maximum of <b>{@H@} hours</b> of labor per day.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>a) </b> Write the two linear inequalities that model the daily timber and labor limitations. <i>(Use \( \le \) or \( \ge \) as appropriate. Do not include the non-negativity constraints).</i></p>

<p>Timber constraint: [[input:ans1]]</p>
<p>Labor constraint: [[input:ans2]]</p>

<div><b>Timber check:</b> [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<div><b>Labor check:</b> [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> The workshop receives a large wholesale order and commits to producing exactly <b>{@x0@} chairs</b> today.</p>
<p>Based on your two inequalities, what is the absolute <b>maximum number of tables</b> (\(y\)) they can also build today without exceeding <b>either</b> the timber or the labor limits?</p>

<p>Maximum possible tables: [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div><b>Check:</b> [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: Formulating the System</b></p>
<p>1. <b>Timber Constraint:</b> The total length of timber used for chairs (\({@A@}x\)) and tables (\({@B@}y\)) cannot exceed the \({@W@}\) meters available.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta1@}} \)</p>

<p>2. <b>Labor Constraint:</b> The total hours of labor used for chairs (\({@C_coef@}x\)) and tables (\({@D@}y\)) cannot exceed the \({@H@}\) hours available.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta2@}} \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Evaluating the Production Bottleneck</b></p>
<p>We are given that \( x = {@x0@} \). To find the maximum number of tables (\(y\)), we must substitute this value into <b>both</b> constraints to see which resource runs out first.</p>

<p><b>1. Checking the Timber Limit:</b><br>
Substitute \( x = {@x0@} \) into \( {@A@}x + {@B@}y \le {@W@} \):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
{@A@}({@x0@}) + {@B@}y &amp;\le {@W@} \\
{@A * x0@} + {@B@}y &amp;\le {@W@} \\
{@B@}y &amp;\le {@W - A*x0@} \\
y &amp;\le {@limit_T@}
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>Based <i>only</i> on timber, they could build up to {@limit_T@} tables.</p>

<p><b>2. Checking the Labor Limit:</b><br>
Substitute \( x = {@x0@} \) into \( {@C_coef@}x + {@D@}y \le {@H@} \):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
{@C_coef@}({@x0@}) + {@D@}y &amp;\le {@H@} \\
{@C_coef * x0@} + {@D@}y &amp;\le {@H@} \\
{@D@}y &amp;\le {@H - C_coef*x0@} \\
y &amp;\le {@limit_L@}
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>Based <i>only</i> on labor, they could build up to {@limit_L@} tables.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b><br>
Because the workshop cannot exceed <i>either</i> limit, we must choose the most restrictive constraint (the smaller number). The production bottleneck limits the maximum number of tables to <b>{@ta3@}</b>.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
